WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:04.929
We launched with product in market

00:00:04.929 --> 00:00:07.560
in January of 2020.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.990
We had imagined doing events and

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:10.980
concerts and

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.962
selling in restaurants.

00:00:12.962 --> 00:00:16.276
All of that disappeared overnight.

00:00:16.276 --> 00:00:18.220
We found ourselves with

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.136
about 40,000 cans of wine and

00:00:21.136 --> 00:00:23.473
no way to get them out there.

00:00:23.473 --> 00:00:28.140
[MUSIC]

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.398
My background is actually not in

00:00:30.398 --> 00:00:30.903
wine.

00:00:30.903 --> 00:00:33.138
I was previously in healthcare and

00:00:33.138 --> 00:00:34.068
consulting.

00:00:34.068 --> 00:00:35.779
&gt;&gt; I worked in corporate strategy

00:00:35.779 --> 00:00:37.432
and corporate development.

00:00:37.432 --> 00:00:39.057
&gt;&gt; I worked in marketing in

00:00:39.057 --> 00:00:40.200
San Francisco.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.852
I did get burn out in technology,

00:00:42.852 --> 00:00:44.937
and wanted to do something

00:00:44.937 --> 00:00:45.989
different.

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:47.298
&gt;&gt; While at Stanford,

00:00:47.298 --> 00:00:49.069
I met my now co-founder and

00:00:49.069 --> 00:00:50.534
best friend, Sarah.

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:52.624
&gt;&gt; I had asked her to be my partner

00:00:52.624 --> 00:00:54.883
for this class startup garage to

00:00:54.883 --> 00:00:56.892
explore the canned wine idea

00:00:56.892 --> 00:00:59.403
because she was the smartest person

00:00:59.403 --> 00:01:00.336
that I knew.

00:01:00.336 --> 00:01:01.939
[LAUGH] &gt;&gt; We spent six months

00:01:01.939 --> 00:01:03.461
meeting with wineries,

00:01:03.461 --> 00:01:05.272
ideating on our concept, and

00:01:05.272 --> 00:01:06.585
our business model.

00:01:06.585 --> 00:01:08.317
What this allowed us to do

00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:10.460
was pressure test our ideas and

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.768
start to build up a network in wine

00:01:12.768 --> 00:01:14.672
in the startup ecosystem.

00:01:14.672 --> 00:01:16.354
It was roughly six months

00:01:16.354 --> 00:01:18.675
later when we were first connected

00:01:18.675 --> 00:01:21.167
to now our third co-founder, Zoe.

00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:22.555
&gt;&gt; Through the GSB network,

00:01:22.555 --> 00:01:24.070
I was introduced to Sarah and

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:24.584
Kendra.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.317
&gt;&gt; As soon as we started working

00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:28.412
together, we knew that Zoe needed

00:01:28.412 --> 00:01:29.866
to be a part of our team.

00:01:29.866 --> 00:01:32.546
&gt;&gt; Now the three of us really

00:01:32.546 --> 00:01:34.594
are inseparable.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.275
[MUSIC]

00:01:41.716 --> 00:01:43.641
&gt;&gt; Maker Wine is a new kind of wine

00:01:43.641 --> 00:01:44.338
company.

00:01:44.338 --> 00:01:47.400
We partner with small independent

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.562
wineries and

00:01:48.562 --> 00:01:51.208
we can their flagship wines.

00:01:51.208 --> 00:01:55.349
[MUSIC]

00:01:55.349 --> 00:01:57.611
We started Maker to create a new

00:01:57.611 --> 00:02:00.134
kind of company that shined a light

00:02:00.134 --> 00:02:02.572
on small independent wineries.

00:02:02.572 --> 00:02:04.705
They are farmers and artists and

00:02:04.705 --> 00:02:06.838
scientists, not marketers and

00:02:06.838 --> 00:02:08.971
salespeople or distributors.

00:02:08.971 --> 00:02:10.538
&gt;&gt; We're working very hard

00:02:10.538 --> 00:02:12.477
to keep the little guys alive so

00:02:12.477 --> 00:02:14.492
that we always get to experience

00:02:14.492 --> 00:02:15.846
this amazing craft.

00:02:15.846 --> 00:02:17.565
&gt;&gt; And then we started to think

00:02:17.565 --> 00:02:19.659
about what type of experience was

00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:21.829
missing as millennial wine lovers

00:02:21.829 --> 00:02:24.073
and wine consumers ourselves, and

00:02:24.073 --> 00:02:26.166
that's when we really had the aha

00:02:26.166 --> 00:02:27.391
moment with cans.

00:02:31.028 --> 00:02:33.410
&gt;&gt; Why has wine always been in

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.662
these super stuffy unapproachable

00:02:36.662 --> 00:02:38.730
heavy glass bottles?

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.749
&gt;&gt; Super old school outdated.

00:02:40.749 --> 00:02:42.874
&gt;&gt; The label tells you nothing,

00:02:42.874 --> 00:02:44.745
not technology forward,

00:02:44.745 --> 00:02:46.615
you never know if the price

00:02:46.615 --> 00:02:48.667
is indicative of the quality

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:50.201
[MUSIC]

00:02:50.201 --> 00:02:52.066
&gt;&gt; I have a passion and background

00:02:52.066 --> 00:02:53.788
in the craft beer industry,

00:02:53.788 --> 00:02:55.797
and saw cans really take over that

00:02:55.797 --> 00:02:57.018
industry because of

00:02:57.018 --> 00:02:58.971
the sustainability benefits.

00:02:58.971 --> 00:03:00.791
&gt;&gt; A lot of people don't know that

00:03:00.791 --> 00:03:02.542
glass is actually very hard to

00:03:02.542 --> 00:03:03.180
recycle.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.356
It's recycled in just about 40% of

00:03:05.356 --> 00:03:07.058
recycling facilities across

00:03:07.058 --> 00:03:08.221
the United States.

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:10.362
&gt;&gt; The aluminum format also weighs

00:03:10.362 --> 00:03:12.506
much less than a bottle of wine.

00:03:12.506 --> 00:03:14.285
So if you think about the carbon

00:03:14.285 --> 00:03:16.134
emissions, transporting wine

00:03:16.134 --> 00:03:18.119
across the world, much better for

00:03:18.119 --> 00:03:19.563
the environment there.

00:03:19.563 --> 00:03:21.628
&gt;&gt; All of those reasons really got

00:03:21.628 --> 00:03:23.223
us excited about cans.

00:03:23.223 --> 00:03:26.232
We actually canned our first three

00:03:26.232 --> 00:03:29.034
wines less than two months after

00:03:29.034 --> 00:03:30.285
graduation.

00:03:30.285 --> 00:03:31.815
It was a wild time to

00:03:31.815 --> 00:03:33.349
launch a company.

00:03:33.349 --> 00:03:35.384
We really had to get creative in

00:03:35.384 --> 00:03:37.497
how we launched a new type of wine

00:03:37.497 --> 00:03:40.016
product during the global pandemic.

00:03:40.016 --> 00:03:42.416
And candidly, the GSB and greater

00:03:42.416 --> 00:03:44.817
Stanford community was a huge part

00:03:44.817 --> 00:03:47.312
of what helped us make that happen.

00:03:47.312 --> 00:03:50.036
What we started doing was hosting

00:03:50.036 --> 00:03:52.079
virtual wine tastings,

00:03:52.079 --> 00:03:54.705
and we did more than 150 in our

00:03:54.705 --> 00:03:57.428
launch year, and so it was really

00:03:57.428 --> 00:04:00.249
leaning on the network that helped

00:04:00.249 --> 00:04:02.877
ultimately get us off the ground

00:04:02.877 --> 00:04:05.237
during a really tough time.

00:04:05.237 --> 00:04:07.177
&gt;&gt; Danford GSB definitely changed

00:04:07.177 --> 00:04:07.756
my life.

00:04:07.756 --> 00:04:09.550
I would not be here today as a CEO

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:11.137
of Maker if I hadn't gone to

00:04:11.137 --> 00:04:13.138
the GSB and met my co-founders and

00:04:13.138 --> 00:04:14.794
met the people who backed our

00:04:14.794 --> 00:04:16.864
business from the very beginning.

00:04:16.864 --> 00:04:18.539
I don't know that I would have

00:04:18.539 --> 00:04:20.482
started this business without the

00:04:20.482 --> 00:04:22.359
confidence that the GSB gave me.

00:04:22.359 --> 00:04:24.159
You walk out of there and

00:04:24.159 --> 00:04:26.863
they feel like you can do anything.

00:04:26.863 --> 00:04:29.170
&gt;&gt; I chose the GSB because I wanted

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.653
to be in a community of people with

00:04:31.653 --> 00:04:34.139
not only business smarts but that

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:36.906
were really conscientious humans.

00:04:36.906 --> 00:04:39.183
&gt;&gt; Everyone that I've met that went

00:04:39.183 --> 00:04:41.624
to GSB was passionate excited doing

00:04:41.624 --> 00:04:43.503
something really unique.

00:04:43.503 --> 00:04:46.031
I wanted to be around those people

00:04:46.031 --> 00:04:48.650
and be inspired to build something

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:49.561
great too.

00:04:49.561 --> 00:04:51.426
&gt;&gt; The motto of the GSB is changing

00:04:51.426 --> 00:04:53.581
lives, changing organizations,

00:04:53.581 --> 00:04:54.744
change the world.

00:04:54.744 --> 00:04:56.901
On the whole, why we are building

00:04:56.901 --> 00:04:59.290
Maker, why we consider ourselves as

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:01.369
a mission driven wine company,

00:05:01.369 --> 00:05:03.615
is so that we can make wine better.

00:05:03.615 --> 00:05:06.196
&gt;&gt; I really want Maker to be this

00:05:06.196 --> 00:05:08.457
incredible platform for

00:05:08.457 --> 00:05:11.149
these independent producers

00:05:11.149 --> 00:05:14.055
to get to highlight minority and

00:05:14.055 --> 00:05:16.315
women owned wineries and

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:19.436
make those stories more visible,

00:05:19.436 --> 00:05:21.822
I think has had an impact.

00:05:21.822 --> 00:05:26.472
&gt;&gt; When we are successful wine as

00:05:26.472 --> 00:05:30.936
an industry, really should

00:05:30.936 --> 00:05:35.404
put the human at the center.

00:05:35.404 --> 00:05:38.964
[MUSIC]

